    "evidence_text": "These hallmarks are: genomic instability, telomere attrition, epigenetic alterations, loss of proteostasis, deregulated nutrient sensing, mitochondrial dysfunction, cellular senescence, stem cell exhaustion, and altered intercellular communication.\n\nTo understand the cell and molecular basis of aging is to unravel the multiplicity of mechanisms causing damage to accumulate and the complex array of systems working to keep damage at bay.\n\nPerceived age, controlled for chronological age and sex, also correlated significantly with physical and cognitive functioning as well as with leucocyte telomere length.   \n\n\np16INK4A is a robust in vivo biomarker of cellular aging in human skin.\n\nIn conclusion, we provide for the first time evidence that p16INK4A expression directly correlates with chronological aging of human skin in vivo.\n\nThe data reported here suggest a model for changes in regulatory gene expression that drive aging in human skin.\n\nThese epigenomic changes are not only observed during aging but also profoundly affect cellular function and stress resistance, thereby contributing to the progression of aging.\n\nCell nuclei from old individuals acquire defects similar to those of HGPS patient cells, including changes in histone modifications and increased DNA damage.\n\nThese observations implicate lamin A in physiological aging.",
